Subject: add vendor and /run tiers to the fuse.conf policy search
Source: downstream patch for fuse3 3.17.4
Upstream: not submitted; needs agreement on the /usr/share/fuse3 and
  /run/fuse3 paths

util/meson.build already builds FUSE_CONF from
join_paths(get_option('prefix'), get_option('sysconfdir'), 'fuse.conf'),
and Meson's FHS special case resolves that to the absolute /etc/fuse.conf
whenever prefix is /usr, so the administrator tier util/fusermount.c's
read_conf() opened was already correctly placed. What was missing was a
vendor tier and a /run tier: a deployment could not ship a default mount
policy and a boot could not override one.

fusermount3 is a privileged mount helper: fuse.conf carries
user_allow_other, which decides whether an unprivileged caller may expose
a mount to other users, and mount_max, which caps how many mounts one
caller may hold. Both new tiers stay root-owned directories an
unprivileged user cannot write (/usr/share/fuse3 and /run/fuse3), no
environment variable is added or honoured to redirect either path, and
neither tier is $HOME or otherwise per-user.

read_conf()'s single-tier body is factored into read_conf_file(path), then
called three times: FUSE_CONF_VENDOR (/usr/share/fuse3/fuse.conf), then
FUSE_CONF_RUN (/run/fuse3/fuse.conf), then the existing FUSE_CONF
(/etc/fuse.conf) last. parse_line() assigns user_allow_other and
mount_max unconditionally whenever it recognizes a keyword, with no
"already set" guard, so whichever tier sets a given keyword *last*
overwrites whatever an earlier tier set: reading the vendor tier first and
the administrator's FUSE_CONF tier last means an administrator value beats
a boot value, which beats a vendor default, for the same key. A tier whose
file is simply absent (ENOENT) stays silent exactly as a missing
/etc/fuse.conf always has, leaving whatever a lower tier already set
untouched.

A tier that exists but cannot be opened for any other reason (permission
denied, a path component that is not a directory, and so on) is recorded
in the new conf_read_error flag rather than silently trusting whatever the
other tiers parsed. read_conf() checks that flag once every tier has been
attempted and, if it was ever set, discards any value any tier parsed and
re-applies the compiled-in defaults (user_allow_other disabled, mount_max
at FUSE_MOUNT_MAX_DEFAULT). This keeps a broken or inaccessible tier from
ever producing a result more permissive than today's single-tier
behavior, where such an open failure already left both settings at their
compiled defaults; in particular it stops a lower tier's successful
"enable" from surviving when a higher tier's file exists but could not be
read. A genuine stream read error (ferror()) still exits the whole program
immediately, exactly as it always has.

--- a/util/fusermount.c	2026-08-19 00:59:25.731260570 -0400
+++ b/util/fusermount.c	2026-08-19 00:59:57.417318350 -0400
@@ -48,0 +49,13 @@
+/* FUSE_CONF (compiled from get_option('prefix')/get_option('sysconfdir'),
+ * which Meson resolves to /etc/fuse.conf) stays the administrator tier.
+ * These two add the vendor and boot tiers below /usr and /run, both
+ * root-owned directories an unprivileged mount caller cannot write. */
+#ifndef FUSE_CONF_VENDOR
+#define FUSE_CONF_VENDOR "/usr/share/fuse3/fuse.conf"
+#endif
+#ifndef FUSE_CONF_RUN
+#define FUSE_CONF_RUN "/run/fuse3/fuse.conf"
+#endif
+
+#define FUSE_MOUNT_MAX_DEFAULT 1000
+
@@ -50 +63 @@
-static int mount_max = 1000;
+static int mount_max = FUSE_MOUNT_MAX_DEFAULT;
@@ -625 +638,12 @@
-static void parse_line(char *line, int linenum)
+/* Set when read_conf_file() hits a tier whose file exists but could not be
+ * read for a reason other than plain absence (permission denied, a path
+ * component that turned out not to be a directory, and so on). read_conf()
+ * checks this after every tier has been attempted and, if it was ever set,
+ * discards whatever any tier parsed and re-applies the compiled-in defaults.
+ * This keeps a broken or inaccessible tier from ever producing a result more
+ * permissive than today's single-/etc/fuse.conf-tier behavior, where such an
+ * open failure already left user_allow_other and mount_max at their
+ * compiled defaults. */
+static bool conf_read_error = false;
+
+static void parse_line(const char *path, char *line, int linenum)
@@ -635 +659 @@
-			progname, FUSE_CONF, linenum, line);
+			progname, path, linenum, line);
@@ -638 +662,6 @@
-static void read_conf(void)
+/* Read one configuration tier's file. A missing file (ENOENT) is silent, as
+ * it already was for the single /etc/fuse.conf tier this is factored out
+ * of. Any other open failure is recorded in conf_read_error so read_conf()
+ * can fail closed once every tier has been attempted; a genuine stream read
+ * error still exits immediately, exactly as it always has. */
+static void read_conf_file(const char *path)
@@ -640 +669 @@
-	FILE *fp = fopen(FUSE_CONF, "r");
+	FILE *fp = fopen(path, "r");
@@ -649 +678 @@
-					parse_line(line, linenum);
+					parse_line(path, line, linenum);
@@ -654 +683 @@
-				fprintf(stderr, "%s: reading %s: line %i too long\n", progname, FUSE_CONF, linenum);
+				fprintf(stderr, "%s: reading %s: line %i too long\n", progname, path, linenum);
@@ -662 +691 @@
-			fprintf(stderr, "%s: reading %s: missing newline at end of file\n", progname, FUSE_CONF);
+			fprintf(stderr, "%s: reading %s: missing newline at end of file\n", progname, path);
@@ -666 +695 @@
-			fprintf(stderr, "%s: reading %s: read failed\n", progname, FUSE_CONF);
+			fprintf(stderr, "%s: reading %s: read failed\n", progname, path);
@@ -675 +704 @@
-			progname, FUSE_CONF, strerror(errno));
+			progname, path, strerror(errno));
@@ -677,0 +707,30 @@
+		conf_read_error = true;
+	}
+}
+
+static void read_conf(void)
+{
+	/* Read the vendor tier, then the boot tier, then the administrator's
+	 * FUSE_CONF (/etc/fuse.conf) last. parse_line() assigns
+	 * user_allow_other and mount_max unconditionally whenever it
+	 * recognizes a keyword, with no "already set" guard, so whichever
+	 * tier sets a given keyword *last* is the value that survives:
+	 * reading the vendor tier first and the administrator tier last
+	 * means an administrator value beats a boot value, which beats a
+	 * vendor default, for the same key. A tier whose file is simply
+	 * absent leaves whatever a lower tier already set untouched, exactly
+	 * as a missing /etc/fuse.conf always has. */
+	conf_read_error = false;
+
+	read_conf_file(FUSE_CONF_VENDOR);
+	read_conf_file(FUSE_CONF_RUN);
+	read_conf_file(FUSE_CONF);
+
+	if (conf_read_error) {
+		fprintf(stderr,
+			"%s: one of %s, %s, %s could not be read; "
+			"enforcing compiled defaults (user_allow_other disabled, mount_max=%i)\n",
+			progname, FUSE_CONF_VENDOR, FUSE_CONF_RUN, FUSE_CONF,
+			FUSE_MOUNT_MAX_DEFAULT);
+		user_allow_other = 0;
+		mount_max = FUSE_MOUNT_MAX_DEFAULT;
